org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sint Anna Bay","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":12.1097,"lo":-68.9326,"d":584,"ds":"Sint Anna Bay (Dutch: Sint Annabaai; Papiamento: Bahia di Santa Ana) is a deep channel approximately one mile long and up to 1,000 feet wide, located on the island of Curaçao between the two parts of Willemstad, Punda and Otrobanda. The bay opens into the Caribbean Sea at the southern end, and into the Schottegat lagoon/industrial area to the north.","wpu":"Sint Anna Bay","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b9/Curacaobridges.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Queen Juliana Bridge","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":12.111111,"lo":-68.931944,"d":756,"ds":"Queen Juliana Bridge (Dutch: Koningin Julianabrug, Papiamento: Brug di Reina Juliana) is a four-lane road bridge across St. Anna Bay in Willemstad, the capital of Caribbean island country Curaçao, which is part of The Kingdom of The Netherlands. The bridge is named after Juliana of the Netherlands.","wpu":"Queen Juliana Bridge","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Curacao_%2826300530245%29.jpg/960px-Curacao_%2826300530245%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Queen of the Most Holy Rosary Cathedral, Willemstad","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":12.1031,"lo":-68.9247,"d":1139,"ds":"The Queen of the Most Holy Rosary Cathedral also known as Pietermaai Cathedral or the Cathedral of the Holy Rosary (Papiamento: Kathedrale Reina di Santisimo Rosario; Dutch: Heilige Rozenkrans kathedraal) is a religious building belonging to the Catholic Church, which serves as the cathedral of the Diocese of Willemstad (Latin: Dioecesis Gulielmopolitana) in Juliana Plein 5, Pietermaai, Willemstad a city on the islan.","wpu":"Queen of the Most Holy Rosary Cathedral, Willemstad","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Cathedral_in_Willemstad%2C_Cura%C3%A7ao.jpg/960px-Cathedral_in_Willemstad%2C_Cura%C3%A7ao.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fort Nassau (Curaçao)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":12.1148,"lo":-68.9268,"d":1407,"ds":"Fort Nassau (officially: Fort Oranje Nassau) is a fort situated in Willemstad, Curaçao. It is located on a 68 metres (223 ft) high hill overlooking the city of Willemstad and its harbour. It was constructed in 1796 as Fort Republiek.","wpu":"Fort Nassau (Curaçao)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Fort_Nassau%2C_Curacao.jpg/960px-Fort_Nassau%2C_Curacao.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Omar bin Al-Khattab Mosque","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":12.1155,"lo":-68.94906,"d":1923,"ds":"The Omar bin Al-Khattab Mosque or Curaçao Islamic Center is a mosque in Willemstad, Curaçao, part of the Kingdom of the Netherlands.","wpu":"Omar bin Al-Khattab Mosque","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Moscura%C3%A7ao.jpg/960px-Moscura%C3%A7ao.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Isla Refinery","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":12.131667,"lo":-68.932222,"d":2980,"ds":"The Isla Refinery (also known as the Shell Refinery) was a Royal Dutch Shell refinery built in 1915 in the deep-water harbor of Curaçao called Schottegat. The refinery allowed access to process crude oil from the Venezuelan Maracabio Basin. It went out of commission on December 31, 2019, due to heavy American sanctions on Venezuela and a lack of crude feedstock.","wpu":"Isla Refinery","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Laika_ac_Isla_Oil_Refinery_%2811693347275%29.jpg/960px-Laika_ac_Isla_Oil_Refinery_%2811693347275%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Landhuis Bloemhof","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":12.11934,"lo":-68.90129,"d":3996,"ds":"Landhuis Bloemhof is a plantation house, art gallery and museum in Willemstad, Curaçao. The house was built in 1735 as Landhuis Nooitgedacht. During the 20th century, the house was owned by sculptor and writer May Henriquez and became a meeting place for the art scene of Curaçao.","wpu":"Landhuis Bloemhof","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Oude_ingang_van_Landhuis_Bloemhof.jpg/960px-Oude_ingang_van_Landhuis_Bloemhof.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Seaquarium Beach","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":12.086111,"lo":-68.898611,"d":4479,"ds":"Seaquarium Beach (also called Sea Aquarium Beach) is a beach on the Caribbean island of Curaçao, located to the south of Willemstad. The beach is named after Curaçao Sea Aquarium, located nearby. The beach is open to the public, but an access fee must be paid.","wpu":"Seaquarium Beach","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9f/Wilhemstad_from_the_coast.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Jan Thiel Lagoon, Curaçao","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":12.090556,"lo":-68.883889,"d":5784,"ds":"Jan Thiel Lagoon is an 80 ha area of hypersaline lagoons, surrounded by woodland, on the southern coast of Curaçao, a constituent island nation of the Kingdom of the Netherlands in the Dutch Caribbean. It is adjacent to the capital, Willemstad. The site, comprising the lagoons, woodland and adjacent coastal waters, has been identified as a 432 ha Important Bird Area by BirdLife International.","wpu":"Jan Thiel Lagoon, Curaçao","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Least_Tern_Chicks_Day_2.jpg/960px-Least_Tern_Chicks_Day_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Muizenberg (Curaçao)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":12.1575,"lo":-68.918611,"d":6103,"ds":"Muizenberg is a wetland and protected area on the Dutch Caribbean island of Curaçao. Located within the northern suburbs of the capital, Willemstad, the wetland is surrounded by well-developed suburbs that are among the most densely populated in Curaçao. The majority of the wetland consists of a shallow, artificial lake formed by the Muizenberg dam, built by Shell Curaçao in 1915.","wpu":"Muizenberg (Curaçao)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Pied-billed_Grebe_0561.jpg/960px-Pied-billed_Grebe_0561.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Blue Bay, Curaçao","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":12.134444,"lo":-68.984722,"d":6319,"ds":"Blue Bay (Dutch: Blauwbaai) is a beach on the Caribbean island of Curaçao, located close to the fishermen's village of Sint-Michiel to the north-west of Willemstad. The beach is named after a plantation \"Blaauw\" that was located nearby. There are several restaurants at the beach and golf course.","wpu":"Blue Bay, Curaçao","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Blue_Bay_-_Curacao.jpg/960px-Blue_Bay_-_Curacao.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Santa Rosa, Curaçao","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":12.1169,"lo":-68.8743,"d":6730,"ds":"Santa Rosa is a town on the island of Curaçao in the former Netherlands Antilles. It is to the east of the capital, Willemstad, located inland from the east coast, to the south of Santa Catarina, and is connected to the eastern coastline via a canal. To the southwest lies the settlement of Bapor Kibra, and to the southeast lies Nieuwpoort.","wpu":"Santa Rosa, Curaçao","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Rooms-Katholieke_kerk%2C_overzicht_-_20652715_-_RCE.jpg/960px-Rooms-Katholieke_kerk%2C_overzicht_-_20652715_-_RCE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sint Michiel","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":12.142222,"lo":-68.995833,"d":7801,"ds":"Sint Michiel (also Boca Samí) is a former fishing village in Willemstad lying at the south end of Bullenbaai bay on the west coast of Curaçao. It is located 10 kilometres (6.2 miles) to the northwest of the island's city centre.","wpu":"Sint Michiel","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Collectie_Nationaal_Museum_van_Wereldculturen_TM-20029786_Kunukuhuis_bij_Sint_Michiel_Curacao_Boy_Lawson_%28Fotograaf%29.jpg/960px-Collectie_Nationaal_Museum_van_Wereldculturen_TM-20029786_Kunukuhuis_bij_Sint_Michiel_Curacao_Boy_Lawson_%28Fotograaf%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Malpais-Sint Michiel","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":12.157222,"lo":-68.990278,"d":8356,"ds":"Malpais-Sint Michiel is a 1,100 ha tract of land, a former plantation just north of Sint Michiels Bay, on the southern coast of Curaçao, a constituent island nation of the Kingdom of the Netherlands in the Dutch Caribbean.","wpu":"Malpais-Sint Michiel","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Buladuif.jpg/960px-Buladuif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hato Caves","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":12.1798,"lo":-68.9484,"d":8443,"ds":"Hato Caves are show caves, publicly accessible since 1991 and a popular tourist attraction on the Caribbean island of Curaçao. The caves consist of marine coral limestone, which accumulated over millions of years and after sea levels had dropped were exposed to atmospheric corrosion and karstic processes. They are approximately 240 m (790 ft) long and cover an area of 4,900 m2 (53,000 sq ft) .","wpu":"Hato Caves","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/HATO_CAVES_%2C_CURACAO.jpg/960px-HATO_CAVES_%2C_CURACAO.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Baya Beach","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":12.070556,"lo":-68.861389,"d":8872,"ds":"Baya Beach is one of 38 official beaches on the Caribbean island of Curaçao, situated on a peninsula in the south-east of Caracas Bay next to Fort Beekenburg. It used to be a bunker port of Royal Dutch Shell; some piers and tanks can still be seen. It is a small beach, mainly geared towards water sports.","wpu":"Baya Beach","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Baya_Beach_Curacao_%28213574239%29.jpeg/960px-Baya_Beach_Curacao_%28213574239%29.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Koraal Partir","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":12.1298,"lo":-68.8538,"d":9248,"ds":"Koraal Partir (also Koraal Partier) is a town in located at the northeast of Curaçao. It is situated close to the north coast of the island, and northeast of the capital Willemstad. The village of Brievengat lies to the west, and Sint Jorisbaai lies to the east.","wpu":"Koraal Partir","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Collectie_Nationaal_Museum_van_Wereldculturen_TM-20029889_Landschap_bij_Koraal_Tabak_Curacao_Boy_Lawson_%28Fotograaf%29.jpg/960px-Collectie_Nationaal_Museum_van_Wereldculturen_TM-20029889_Landschap_bij_Koraal_Tabak_Curacao_Boy_Lawson_%28Fotograaf%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Willemstad ( WIL-əm-staht, VIL-; Dutch: [ˈʋɪləmstɑt]; Papiamento: [wiləmˈstad]) is the capital and largest city of Curaçao, an island in the southern Caribbean Sea that is a constituent country of the Kingdom of the Netherlands. It was the capital of the Netherlands Antilles prior to that entity's dissolution in 2010. The city accounts for around 90% of Curaçao's population, with 136,660 inhabitants as of 2011.","wpu":"Willemstad","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Handelskade_in_Willemstad.jpg/960px-Handelskade_in_Willemstad.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}